  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">How to read a published range</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A range on a buyer&rsquo;s homepage is marketing, and it is doing a specific job: setting the top of the funnel wide enough to capture everyone. That does not make it dishonest. It makes it uninformative on its own.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>A band is not an offer</strong>It describes what the buyer says it has paid before, not what it will pay you.</li>
        <li><strong>A calculator is not a valuation</strong>It prices inputs, and the inputs are the ones the buyer chose to ask about.</li>
        <li><strong>A disclosed license is evidence</strong>The strongest number available, and still one deal, at one time, for one dataset.</li>
        <li><strong>A unit rate is a floor, not a price</strong>Per-article rates describe component pricing, before anyone has looked at whether the corpus is coherent.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">The column that matters most in the table above is the fourth. When a number is a band, treat it as a ceiling. When it is a worked example, read the inputs. When it is a disclosed license, ask what the scope was, because the scope is where the value sat.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Why we publish rows we cannot verify</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Because the alternative is worse. Every other page in this market quotes these numbers without saying which are audited, which are advertised and which are examples. A seller who does not know the difference negotiates against the wrong number.</p>
      <p class="reveal">Where a figure came from a third-party collation, we say so. Where a buyer publishes nothing, the row says &ldquo;not published&rdquo; rather than leaving a gap that reads like zero. And where we have no source at all, there is no row.</p>
      <p class="reveal">If a figure here is wrong or out of date, tell us and we will correct it with the date of the correction. See <a href="editorial-standards.html">our editorial standards</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What is missing from every page here</h2>
      <p class="reveal">No buyer publishes its median, its pass rate, or a deletion certificate. No buyer publishes a redacted copy of a signed license. The closest thing to a track record in this market is four anonymised figures on one site, and they cannot be checked.</p>
      <p class="reveal">That is the gap this table exposes, and it is the reason our own market page promises to publish the count, the median, the days to payment and the deals that fell through once we have closed deals.</p>
